 calcium release  1067 was a control compound in DMSO of  courses vehicle as I mentioned mrgp rx 2  in humans is found in mass cells and  mast cells  induce degranulation in in exposure to  allergens and this leads to histamine  release so many of the symptoms that you  or your relatives may experience  seasonal rhinitis colds allergies etc  are due to histamine release from mast  cells it turns out that mast cells are  very useful system to evaluate MRG prx2  because they are expressed endogenously  and so we decided to use the lab 2 cell  which is human mast cell line which  endogenously Russ's mrgp RX 2 and  measured intracellular calcium release  as well as the release of beta hexose a  minute days which is a measure of mast  cell degranulation and when we measured  beta hexosaminidase release you can see  that our our tool compound was  reasonably potent at activating it along  with other compounds that were known to  have activity and interestingly enough  for both plus and minus morphine and  these results were very consistent with  our data that we obtained with  transfected HEK cells and one of the  things that's known about mrgp RX 2 is  that agonist promote degranulation in a  non I GE dependent fashion and that's  basically shown here and finally that  based on all of this information we  proposed that this might be the  mechanism for a previously known and  widely reported opioid drug effect so  it's knowing for instance that people  that take opioids like morphine and so  on in addition to the beneficial effects  of opioids frequently have itching so  it's called pure itis  and this has been known for decades and  recently say the last 20 years or so has  been known to be induced by morphine and  similar medications but not mediated by  canonical opioid receptors and some of  the strongest evidence for that is  because the drug codeine which itself is  inactive it codeine actually has to be  metabolized to morphine and induced mast  cell degranulation so it doesn't affect  opiate receptors but requires g-proteins  so so what we think basically is that  this receptor MRG prx2 is the  long-sought itch receptor mediated which  mediates many of the side effects of  opioids we think that medications that  target this might be very effective for  treating pain and itch as I mentioned  one of the main approaches we're using  now is to use computational docking to  identify new ligands for orphan and  understudy gpcrs i just want to give a  shout-out to my collaborators of  Braunschweig cath lab they have built a  database called zinc it's entirely in  the public domain up until a couple of  years ago it had six million compounds  in stock for docking there now are over  160 million compounds in available in a  few years there will be more than a  billion compounds available for  computational docking so those of you  that are interested in these  technologies please check out their site  sinked org so what I showed you in the  in the first main part of my talk was  how we can use advanced computational  approaches as well as small molecule  drug discovery which is enabled by new  screening technologies to find drug like  compounds for these understudy GPCRs as  you may know over the last few years  there have been a number of GPCRs for  which high resolution crystal structures  have been published many from my lab and  my collaborators and I just like to give  you a brief view on how we might use  these crystal structures to identify a  new ligands what I'm going to do is just  highlight a recent paper that was  published in science just a couple of  months ago where we solved the structure  of the d4 dopamine receptor so dopamine  is a very important neurotransmitter  it's involved in virtually all of the  rewarding stimuli rewarding properties  virtually all stimuli drugs alcohol sex  Twitter and so on this regulation of  dopaminergic neurotransmission is also  involved in a number of diseases  Parkinson's disease other basal ganglia  disorders schizophrenia virtually all  psychiatric disorders there are five  dopamine receptors in the genome if you  go to chemical databases thousands of  compounds are annotated against them in  large numbers of papers in PubMed but  despite all this interest in dopamine  and dopamine receptors creating subtypes  selective Linkens has proved challenging  and what we found when we obtained the  first high-resolution view of a dopamine  receptor was that there and this is just  a summary of that structure down to one  point nine five angstroms was that there  was this interesting water network  stabilizing receptor you can see the  electron density maps here were very  very high indicating very high level  resolution but the interesting thing  from a drug discovery perspective was  that when we compared this structure  with a previous relatively low  resolution structure of related receptor  d3  found that there was a new extended  binding pocket here for the drug that we  used to solve the structure here which  was unique for the d4 receptor and it  was not found in the d3 and I just want  to draw your attention to this because  we last week published in nature the  structure of the d2 receptor and we find  that that this extended binding pocket  is also not found in the d2 receptor so  given the fact that there's this  extended binding pocket it really opened  up the possibility that we could use  this pocket to identify new new and  selective ligands for the d4 receptor so  what was done again we went to our  collaborators Brian showed ket's lab in  this case an app Levin and she talked a  little over a million compounds against  the structure of the d2 receptor in  total this was trillions of complexes  and high high scoring compounds scored  by the docking algorithm were then  obtained and tested and you can see in a  typical screen here on the right around  three of the ten or so compounds so  about a 30% hit rate were obtained these  compounds the best of these compounds  which we call compound 9 was then taken  with an iterative strategy that involved  analog screen and redocking and in total  a number of compounds were obtained for  that screen two distinct chemo types  were found compound 9 - 6 and 9 - 11  these were then as you can see a higher  affinity in this case 30 to 60 nano  molar an analog screen and redocking was  done and this led to our final compound  9 - 6 - 24 which now has a single-digit  animal potency and is ten thousandfold  selective against the d4 receptor so you  can see in a relatively straightforward  process we were able to go from micro  molar compounds to single digit Nana  compounds in this case without any  chemistry all these compounds were  commercially available so the highlights  of this structure it's the highest  resolution structure of a biogenic and  beam receptor the first GPCR that was  crystallized with and without explicit  sodium the extended binding pocket  revealed residues essential for receptor  subtype selectivity and I yielded a  platform which yield a new chemical  matter for the D for dopamine receptor  and so that's all I want to say today I  want to thank you very much for  listening I just want to highlight  people in my lab who did this work the  first part of the talk was done headed  up by Wes sheep ring and the second part  was done by Kate primarily with wes's  help and our collaborators Brian show  Chet and Joel cardiac and the d4  receptor structure was done with Shang  and a Nath Leavitt also wanted knowledge  the funding NIH eliminating the  druggable genome and then Kate had a  grant from the pharma Foundation and  thank you very much I'm happy to answer  any questions you may have  Thank You dr. Roth for that informative  presentation we will now start the live  Q&amp;A portion of the webinar if you have a  question you asked please do so now just  click on the ask a question drop-down  box located on the far left of your  presentation window type your question  into the box that appears on your screen  and click the send button well answer as  many questions as we have time for and  so let's get started our first question  is can you tell me about how to access  the screening program yeah so that's a  good question so the the easiest way to  access it is to simply top type my name  into Google Brian Roth and you'll go to  our website it's the NIH psychoactive  drug screening center and if you if you  basically go there there's a basically a  very nice menu that provides all sorts  of information on on how you might  obtain screening we have an online  database which is has been very popular  we get about a million hits a month and  and other information there now second  the other thing I might I might mention  is that the screening is available to  anyone who is funded by the National  Institute of Health it's paid for with  the grant that we have from the NIH and  screening is entirely free and  confidential so it's a really good deal  so next question our next question is  what are the new frontiers in gpcr drug  discovery so I would say there are two  so one one frontier that I mentioned a  lot in this particular talk is with the  really the revolution that's ongoing and  you can see our structure determination  we're now able to take advantage of  these structures and use very advanced  computational approaches to find new  chemical matter  the receptors and so many many labs are  doing that I think this is a very active  area and as I mentioned currently we're  able to access a basis basically  libraries of small molecules that are  virtual of a hundred hundred plus  million compounds so way more than could  ever be screened in a physical way the  other big area of gpcr research  currently and the frontier is relates to  bias signalling I didn't I didn't go  into that to any extent in the talk but  if you remember at the beginning of the  talk I mentioned there are these broadly  speaking two pathways of GPCR signaling  through g-protein and arresting it's  becoming clear that activating one  pathway over the other may be very  important either for side effects or  therapeutic effects of both approved and  candidate medications and so the idea is  that if we can take make drugs that  target either the arresting pathway or  the g-protein pathway we might be able  to make safer and more effective  medication so I would say that's that's  the other frontier the third of course  there are these understudied GPCRs we  you know don't know anything about them  really any any new information is likely  to be very important going forward and  it looks like we have time for one more  question what if you don't have a GPCR  structure right so as I mentioned if you  don't have a structure what we can do is  we can do computational modeling and as  was exemplified in the talk and one of  the nice things about about that  approach as well as the approaches that  I that I mentioned here is all of these  resources modeler which is used to make  the models sink the zinc library which  is a source of the of the compounds and  then the program dock which is used to  dock the compounds as well as our  screening resources these are all now  available in the public domain without  really any restrictions to use by  academic investigators so  particularly the computational  approaches actually can can be used  remotely so if you if you simply have  access to a PC or a Mac and the Internet  you can you can actually use these  resources to create your own models and  then do the docking you know potentially  in your own in your own home without  without a biopsy is that the technology  is has really become quite user friendly  I would like to once again thank dr.  Roth for his presentation I would also  like to thank leverets for making  today's educational webcast possible  before we go I want to look everyone  know that today's webcast will be  available for on-demand viewing through  May of 2018 you will receive an email  from laberd's letting you know when this  webcast will be available for replay  please share that announcement with your  colleagues who may have missed today's  live event that's all for now thank you  for joining us and we hope to see you  again soon goodbye
 

